    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p33-q1",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Three-letter codes are formed from the letters of the word NEUMANN. Counting the number of possible codes requires considering cases.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. NEUMANN contains three N's; the other four letters are distinct. Separate the codes into cases containing zero, one, two or three N's.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q2",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Two-letter codes are formed from the letters of AAAAAAAB. The probability of AA occurring is $\\frac{1}{3}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True if the three distinct codes AA, AB and BA are equally likely; false if two separate letter cards are selected randomly without replacement.",
          "worked": "If the distinct codes AA, AB and BA are equally likely, $P(\\mathrm{AA})=\\frac{1}{3}$, so the statement is true.\n\nIf the seven A cards and one B card are sampled without replacement, then\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\mathrm{AA})&=\\frac{\\binom{7}{2}}{\\binom{8}{2}}\\\\P(\\mathrm{AA})&=\\frac{21}{28}\\\\P(\\mathrm{AA})&=\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe statement is false under this interpretation.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q3",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "The number of permutations of $r$ objects from $n$ distinct objects is equal to the number of ways to choose $r$ objects, then multiplying by $r!$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True. ${}^{n}P_r=\\binom{n}{r}r!$.",
          "worked": "True. Choose the $r$ objects in $\\binom{n}{r}$ ways, then arrange them in $r!$ ways. Hence,\n$${}^{n}P_r=\\binom{n}{r}r!.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
